--TEST--
An anonymous function's parameter naming a class is checked against the argument's own ancestry at the callable's entry, so an unrelated class refuses by name while a subclass, an interface and every erased spelling accept — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:types/anonymous-function`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*, over the one argument
// shape the neighbouring `arr-a-nullable-callback-parameter-is-checked-against-its-declared-type`
// case cannot reach: an **object**. Two checks stand at a callable's entry and
// the case asserts where each one stops.
//
// `nvs_runtime::callable::check_param_tags` compares the four-bit
// representation a parameter erases to, and `nvs_ir::lower::param_tag_nibble`
// gives every class name — and `object` itself — the same nibble 7, because
// the tag carries no class label. That draws the first line around objecthood:
// every non-object representation is refused before the body runs.
//
// The class label the nibble cannot hold is read from the callable's `invoke`
// row on a call that proved nothing, before the body runs, and again at the
// body's own first block (`nvs_ir::lower::anon_fn::check_param_class`). That
// draws the second line around the argument's *ancestry*, not its exact class.
// The row check reads the whole declared type, so a `?T` is checked as `T` or
// `null`.
// `rule:types/callable-signature` says why the callable's entry is the
// boundary that pays for it: without it a named-class
// binding reads and writes at a fixed offset against a label nothing verified,
// which is a type confusion rather than a wrong answer.
//
// `Core\Out::capture`'s `{through:}` is the vehicle for the first half because
// it is the one `Core` member that hands a callback an object — its own
// `Core\Cli\Text` carrier. A `callable` is opaque as to signature
// (`rule:types/anonymous-function`), so nothing static stands between the option and what the
// callable declares, and these two checks are the whole of what stands there at
// run time.
final class Marker {
    public string $name = "m";
}

// Each declaration is written twice, bare then nullable, so a pair that
// flipped fails rather than balancing a total. `y` is "the callable ran", `n`
// is "the call threw `LogicError`" — and the pairs are the carrier's own class
// first, then the five representations an object is not, then a class the
// argument is not an instance of, then the two erased spellings.
array<callable> $filters = [
    fn (Core\Cli\Text $c) => $c,
    fn (?Core\Cli\Text $c) => $c,
    fn (string $c) => $c,
    fn (?string $c) => $c,
    fn (int $c) => $c,
    fn (?int $c) => $c,
    fn (bool $c) => $c,
    fn (?bool $c) => $c,
    fn (array<string> $c) => $c,
    fn (?array<string> $c) => $c,
    // `float` is the one representation with a widening rule (`rule:types/conversion`),
    // and it is a rule about an `int` or a `uint` arriving — an object is
    // refused here like any other mismatch.
    fn (float $c) => $c,
    fn (?float $c) => $c,
    // The class pair: `Marker` shares no ancestry with the carrier, so both
    // halves are refused.
    fn (Marker $c) => $c,
    fn (?Marker $c) => $c,
    // `rule:types/grammar`'s opaque top, and `mixed`, which admits everything.
    fn (object $c) => $c,
    fn (mixed $c) => $c,
];

var $marks = "";
array<string> $said = [];
foreach ($filters as callable $filter) {
    try {
        var $answer = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "w"; }, {through: $filter});
        $marks = $marks . "y";
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $marks = $marks . "n";
        $said[] = $refused->message;
    }
}
echo $marks, "\n";

// A bare type's refusal names the representation the parameter asked for and
// the one the argument carried. The row check's refusal names the class that
// arrived. The refusals are in the order the sweep met them, so a message that
// grew or lost a side fails here rather than passing a count.
foreach ($said as string $line) {
    echo $line, "\n";
}

// The other half of the class line, which needs a user class on both sides and
// so takes a different vehicle: `nvs_runtime::object::NvsObj::is_instance_of`
// is a flattened ancestry scan, so a parameter naming a **parent** and one
// naming an **interface** both accept the same instance a parameter naming its
// exact class does. The count is what says the callback ran.
class Base {
    public int $n = 1;
}

class Sub extends Base {
}

interface Shown {
    public function shown(): string;
}

final class Shows implements Shown {
    public function shown(): string {
        return "s";
    }
}

array<Sub> $subs = [new Sub()];
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::filter($subs, fn (Sub $s): bool => $s->n == 1)), "\n";
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::filter($subs, fn (Base $b): bool => $b->n == 1)), "\n";

array<Shows> $shows = [new Shows()];
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::filter($shows, fn (Shown $s): bool => $s->shown() == "s")), "\n";

// And the type confusion the entry check exists to close, in the shape it was
// found in: one wrong callback over a list of another final class. Before the
// check this wrote through `Holder`'s `string` slot at the offset `Shows` lays
// out something else in.
//
// Asked through an option bag's `{by:}`, which is a bare `callable`: at
// `filter`, and at every other member whose row writes the callback's
// signature (`rule:types/callable-signature`), the same confusion is refused
// where it is written and never reaches the entry check. That is the check
// being *spent* rather than removed, and this line is what it still answers
// where nothing was proven.
try {
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Arr::unique($shows, {by: fn (Holder $h) => $h->name})), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $confused) {
    echo $confused->message, "\n";
}

final class Holder {
    public string $name = "held";
}

// Nothing the refused rows captured reaches this program's output: the capture
// level is closed on both edges whichever way the `through` goes. What an
// accepted one answers is the carrier itself, unconverted, which is the same
// statement as the `y` above, said where it can be read.
echo Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => { echo "arrives whole under a nullable class"; }, {through: fn (?Core\Cli\Text $c) => $c}), "\n";
--EXPECT--
yynnnnnnnnnnnnyy
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type string, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type int, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type bool, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type array, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` must be of type float, object given
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Core\Cli\Text`
1
1
1
argument 1 to a `callable` does not accept an object of class `Shows`
arrives whole under a nullable class
